The Civilization of Morocco: From the Dawn of Humanity to the Modern Kingdom

A Complex Adaptive Systems Approach to North African Resilience

Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

This study reconceptualizes Moroccan civilization not as a sequence of dynastic regimes but as a complex adaptive system that has demonstrated resilience across more than 300,000 years of human occupation. 

Challenging the conventional historiography that begins Moroccan history with the Idrisid dynasty in 788 CE, the dissertation develops a longue durée framework grounded in archaeology, paleoclimatology, historical linguistics, institutional theory, and primary textual archives. 

The central thesis is that five interlocking mechanisms—geographical corridor connectivity, institutional modularity, juridical and linguistic pluralism, a cultural logic of synthesis, and a political theology of Sharifian legitimacy—account for the system’s capacity to absorb external shocks without losing systemic coherence. 

The argument proceeds through five parts: deep-time foundations from the Middle Pleistocene to the Neolithic; the first synthesis under Phoenician, Carthaginian, and Roman contact; the formation of the Makhzen during the medieval imperial laboratories; the emergence of an Atlantic-Saharan world-system in the early modern period; and modern resilience from the Protectorate to the 2011 Constitution. Methodologically, the study triangulates archaeological data (Jebel Irhoud, Taforalt, Sijilmassa), medieval Arabic geographical texts, Hebrew Geniza documents, Amazigh oral traditions, and colonial archives. 

The dissertation contributes to Moroccan studies by moving beyond dynastic nationalism, to civilizational studies by providing a testable model of resilience, and to resilience theory by offering a deep-time empirical case.

Keywords: Morocco, civilization, complex adaptive systems, longue durée, resilience, Amazigh, Makhzen, trans-Saharan trade, Sharifism, institutional bricolage

