Chapter 2

Climate, Ecology, and the Emergence of Social Complexity

Between approximately 14,500 and 5,000 years before present, the Sahara was not the hyper‑arid barrier that we know today but a mosaic of savannah grasslands, gallery forests, and extensive lake systems—a climatic episode known as the African Humid Period (deMenocal et al. 2000; Kuper and Kröpelin 2006). This was not a marginal fluctuation in precipitation but a systemic transformation of the entire North African ecumene. The desert, for a period of some nine millennia, was largely dissolved as an ecological obstacle, and the demographic and economic possibilities of the region expanded accordingly. Human populations were able to disperse across landscapes that had been uninhabitable during the preceding glacial maximum and would become uninhabitable again once the monsoon belt retreated southward.

The genetic evidence from this period has, in the past decade, fundamentally rewritten the population history of North Africa. The most important contribution from the territory of present‑day Morocco comes from the Iberomaurusian burial site of Taforalt, the Grotte des Pigeons, in the eastern Rif. In 2018, Van de Loosdrecht and colleagues published genome‑wide data from seven individuals dated to between 15,100 and 13,900 calibrated years before present, and the results decisively overturned any simple model of North African isolation (Van de Loosdrecht et al. 2018). The Taforalt population proved to be most closely related to Near Eastern foragers, specifically the Natufians of the Levant, while also carrying roughly one‑third ancestry from a sub‑Saharan African source. Equally significant is what was not found: despite the “Iberomaurusian” label’s misleading implication of a connection to Iberia, the Taforalt individuals show no detectable admixture from western European populations. Their uniparental markers—predominantly Y‑chromosome haplogroup E and mitochondrial haplogroup U—are lineages that remain traceable in North African populations today. The picture that emerges from Taforalt is one of a population already shaped by bidirectional gene flow across the Mediterranean and the Sahara, but with a deep local root that would persist through subsequent millennia.

The most significant recent addition to this archaeogenetic record comes not from Morocco itself but from the Takarkori rock shelter in the Acacus Mountains of southwestern Libya, a site that illuminates the deep interior of the Sahara during the humid phase. In a study published in Nature in 2025, Salem and colleagues recovered ancient genomes from two Pastoral Neolithic women interred approximately 7,000 years ago, in the middle of the African Humid Period (Salem et al. 2025). The results are striking and, in some respects, counter‑intuitive. The majority of Takarkori ancestry derives from a previously unknown North African genetic lineage that diverged from sub‑Saharan African populations at roughly the same time as the ancestors of present‑day non‑Africans left the continent, and which appears to have remained largely isolated throughout its existence. Despite living at a time when the Green Sahara theoretically permitted movement between the Mediterranean littoral and the Sahel, the Takarkori individuals show no substantial genetic ties to sub‑Saharan African populations, and only limited, ancient admixture from Levantine groups. This suggests that the Sahara functioned, in demographic terms, as a barrier even while it was ecologically open, and that pastoralism arrived in the region primarily as a technology and a set of practices transmitted through cultural diffusion rather than through large‑scale population replacement (Salem et al. 2025; cf. Pereira et al. 2025).

The Takarkori findings carry important implications for the corridor thesis developed in the introduction to this dissertation. The “Sahara as bridge” model, which will prove analytically powerful for understanding the historically documented trans‑Saharan trade of the medieval and early modern periods, applies less straightforwardly to Pleistocene and early Holocene population history. In the deep past, the Sahara enabled the movement of goods, technologies, and ideas well before it reliably moved large numbers of people and genes. The corridor was open to cultural transmission, but demographic exchange across it was episodic, selective, and constrained by factors that the genetic evidence is only beginning to reveal. This is not a refutation of the corridor thesis but a refinement: the functions of a geographical corridor—ecological, cultural, demographic—need not operate in lockstep, and their relative importance shifts across different temporal scales and historical contexts.

Crucially, the Takarkori individuals are genetically closely related to the earlier Taforalt foragers of Morocco, establishing a link between the Atlantic‑facing archaeological record and the deep interior of the Sahara. The North African lineage that is visible at Takarkori 7,000 years ago is demonstrably continuous with the Iberomaurusian populations of the Maghreb, and, through them, with the much deeper Pleistocene occupation of the region. This does not mean that the populations of North Africa were static or unchanging—the gene flow from the Levant detected at both Taforalt and Takarkori indicates otherwise—but it does mean that the demographic substrate of the region exhibits a degree of long‑term continuity that is rarely found in other parts of the world that experienced large‑scale Neolithic population replacements.

As the African Humid Period drew to a close after approximately 5,000 years before present, the Sahara began its inexorable return to aridity. The ecological refugia to which human populations retreated—the coastal plains, the Atlas valleys, the oases of the pre‑Saharan zone—became zones of demographic concentration and social intensification. Settlement sizes grew. Subsistence strategies diversified to include a broader range of domesticated crops and livestock. Social stratification became archaeologically visible in differential burial treatment and the circulation of prestige goods. The symbolic and ritual complexity evident in the burial practices at Taforalt and Ifri n’Amr ou Moussa suggests that the populations of this period were engaged in forms of collective identity formation that would, millennia later, crystallise into the institutional structures examined in subsequent parts of this dissertation. The genetic data now available allows this narrative to be stated with greater precision than was possible a decade ago: the populations that intensified after 5000 BP were not newcomers replacing an earlier stock, but the descendants of a genetically distinctive North African lineage with continuous local roots stretching back through the Iberomaurusian and, per the Takarkori evidence, deep into the Pleistocene.

