Chapter 3

The Amazigh Substratum — Linguistics, Genetics, and Cultural Continuity

The Amazigh languages, conventionally designated as Berber, constitute one of the six primary branches of the Afro‑Asiatic language phylum. They are spoken today by communities scattered across an immense geographical arc extending from the Siwa Oasis in western Egypt through Libya, Tunisia, Algeria, and Morocco to Mauritania, with the highest demographic concentration in Morocco. Estimates of the current speaker population vary considerably depending on methodology, definitional criteria, and political context, ranging from approximately 14 million (Ethnologue) to as many as 25 million when diaspora communities and semi‑speakers are included. This numerical imprecision is itself symptomatic of the condition of a language family that has, for most of its history, been more spoken than written, and whose speakers are frequently multilingual, under‑counted in national censuses, and reluctant to declare their linguistic identity in political environments that have not always been hospitable.

A critical distinction, essential to the chronological framework of this dissertation, must be drawn between the age of the Afro‑Asiatic phylum as a whole and the age of the Berber branch specifically. The two operate on profoundly different timescales. Proto‑Afro‑Asiatic, the hypothetical common ancestor of Semitic, Egyptian, Cushitic, Chadic, Omotic, and Berber, is conventionally dated by glottochronological and lexical‑cultural reconstruction to somewhere between 15,000 and 10,000 BCE (Ehret 1995; Militarev 2005). This estimate, it must be acknowledged, carries substantial uncertainty, since the internal subgrouping of the six Afro‑Asiatic branches remains a matter of active dispute and the time‑depths involved push against the reliable limits of the comparative method. Proto‑Berber, by contrast, is comparatively shallow. Linguists place its diversification into the modern Berber languages—Tashelhit, Tarifit, Central Atlas Tamazight, Kabyle, Tuareg, and the rest—at a time depth comparable to the divergence of the Romance or Germanic subfamilies within Indo‑European (Kossmann 2013). That is to say, on the order of two millennia, not fifteen. This means that the linguistic Berber identity as we now recognise it is a comparatively recent crystallisation, superimposed atop a much older Afro‑Asiatic‑speaking presence in North Africa, which is itself superimposed atop the far deeper genetic continuities discussed in Chapter 2. The linguistic substratum is not a single, undifferentiated layer but a stratigraphy of successive transformations, each of which reworked inherited material without obliterating it.

The genetic signature of the Amazigh‑speaking populations adds another dimension to this picture of layered continuity, though it must be handled with the same conceptual caution. The Y‑chromosome haplogroup E‑M81 (also designated E1b1b1b) is the dominant paternal lineage among Amazigh communities, reaching frequencies as high as 70 to 80 per cent in some relatively isolated populations of the Atlas and the Rif (Cruciani et al. 2004; Arredi et al. 2004). The geographical distribution of this haplogroup, with its peak in the Maghreb and its traceable spillover into southern Europe via Phoenician and Punic seafaring, Roman‑era mobility, and the Islamic expansion into Iberia, makes it a genuinely useful marker for tracing the demographic footprint of North African populations across the Mediterranean basin (Semino et al. 2004; Fregel et al. 2018).

Its point of origin, however, remains the subject of active disagreement, and this dissertation should not attempt to paper over the discrepancy. Some ancient‑DNA evidence from Neolithic burials in the Maghreb suggests that E‑M81 was already present in the region by the mid‑Holocene, consistent with a long and largely indigenous history (Fregel et al. 2018). A separate whole‑Y‑chromosome sequencing study, however, calculated a time‑to‑most‑recent‑common‑ancestor for the dominant subclade of E‑M81 at only roughly 2,000 to 3,000 years before present, indicating an extremely recent and rapid expansion rather than a deep Paleolithic one (Solé‑Morata et al. 2017). These findings are not necessarily contradictory, but they operate at different levels of phylogenetic resolution. The deeper ancestry of the E‑M81 clade as a whole may well trace back to the early Holocene or earlier, while the particular subclade that today dominates the Moroccan landscape may represent a much more recent demographic pulse—possibly associated with the same processes of Arabisation, Islamisation, and state formation that transformed the Maghreb during the medieval period. The broader point, and the one that matters for this dissertation’s thesis, is that genetic data support long‑term North African population continuity in general terms while simultaneously revealing a complex mosaic of ancestries shaped by Neolithic expansions, Phoenician and Roman colonisation, Arab conquest, trans‑Saharan trade, the expulsion of the Moriscos, and later European colonialism (Henn et al. 2012; Arauna et al. 2017). The Amazigh genetic signature is real, but it is not a fossilised remainder of an unchanging ancestral population. It is, like all human genetic landscapes, a palimpsest.

The linguistic record independently confirms the synthesis thesis that is central to this dissertation. Arabic loanwords now account for approximately 35 to 46 per cent of the vocabulary in Kabyle and roughly 52 per cent in Tarifit (Kossmann 2013; Chaker 1996). This is a striking degree of lexical penetration, yet it has left the underlying Berber grammatical architecture—its root‑and‑pattern morphology, inherited from Afro‑Asiatic—largely intact. Berber phonology, verbal morphology, and core syntax remain recognisably Berber, even in varieties that have borrowed heavily from Arabic. This is institutional bricolage operating at the level of language itself: the absorption of massive quantities of exogenous material without systemic replacement. The Amazigh languages have not been effaced by Arabic; they have incorporated Arabic elements into a structure that remains distinctively their own. The result is not a transitional pidgin or a mixed language but a set of Amazigh dialects that are thoroughly, complexly bilingual in their lexical architecture while remaining structurally Amazigh.

The modern institutional chapter of this substratum begins with the founding of the Institut Royal de la Culture Amazighe (IRCAM) by royal decree on 17 October 2001, under the reign of King Mohammed VI. IRCAM was charged with safeguarding and promoting Amazigh language and culture within the framework of the Moroccan state, a mandate that represented a dramatic reversal of decades of official Arabisation policy that had marginalised Amazigh speech and rendered it effectively invisible in the public sphere (Errihani 2006). IRCAM’s first major technical achievement was the standardisation of a modern Neo‑Tifinagh orthography, formally decreed as the official script for Amazigh in February 2003, a 33‑character system that subsequently shaped the Unicode Tifinagh block adopted in 2005 (Ameur et al. 2005). The line from the ancient Libyco‑Berber inscriptions of the Sahara and the Atlas to the digital typography of the twenty‑first century is direct and traceable. This institutional revival culminates, in legal terms, in the 2011 constitutional recognition of Tamazight as an official language of the Kingdom alongside Arabic, a development whose full political and institutional implications are examined in Part V of this dissertation. Its significance here, at the close of Part I, is to provide a contemporary terminus for the long trajectory that this chapter has traced: from Proto‑Afro‑Asiatic speech communities of the early Holocene, through the Iberomaurusian foragers of Taforalt, to the modern institutional apparatus of a state that has formally recognised the linguistic substratum it once sought to erase.

