Synthesis for Part I

The three chapters that constitute Part I have established, on independent but mutually reinforcing evidentiary tracks—palaeoanthropological, archaeogenetic, and linguistic—the following propositions.

First, the territory of present‑day Morocco has supported a continuously evolving, internally differentiated human population for at least 300,000 years. The mosaic anatomy of the Jebel Irhoud fossils demonstrates that this region participated, from the earliest documentable moment, in the pan‑African evolutionary processes that produced Homo sapiens. Second, the genetic record, anchored by the Taforalt and Takarkori genomes, reveals a North African lineage with demonstrable continuity from the Iberomaurusian through the Green Sahara pastoralists and into the historical period, overlaid but not erased by gene flow from the Levant, sub‑Saharan Africa, and, later, Europe. Third, the Amazigh linguistic stratum, though comparatively young in its present configuration, represents the most recent crystalline layer of a much older Afro‑Asiatic‑speaking presence, and its modern institutional recognition through IRCAM and the 2011 Constitution marks the latest phase of a long history of cultural persistence under conditions of intense external contact.

Together, these findings establish the foundational premise upon which the remainder of this dissertation rests. When Phoenician sailors established their first trading posts along the Moroccan coast in the early first millennium BCE, when Roman administrators incorporated Mauretania Tingitana into the imperial system, when Arab armies crossed the Maghreb in the seventh century CE, and when French colonial forces imposed the Protectorate in 1912—none of these arrivals was an encounter with an empty landscape. Each was an engagement with a population that possessed its own deep temporal roots, its own linguistic and cultural repertoires, and its own long history of absorbing, adapting, and indigenising external contact. The civilisation that would emerge from these successive encounters was built not on a blank slate but on a palimpsest of extraordinary depth. Part II now turns to the first of those encounters in the historical record: the arrival of Phoenician commerce and the beginnings of the synthesis that would define Moroccan civilisation for the next three millennia.


PART II

THE SYNTHESIS BEGINS (1000 BCE – 700 CE)

---

Introduction to Part II: Encounter Without Erasure

Part I of this dissertation established the deep-time foundations of Moroccan civilisation, demonstrating that the territory of the Maghreb al-Aqsa supported continuously evolving human populations for at least 300,000 years, and that the Amazigh linguistic and genetic substratum represents a palimpsest of extraordinary depth rather than a blank slate awaiting external inscription. Part II now turns to the first historically documented cycle of intensive external contact—the successive incursions of Phoenician, Carthaginian, and Roman actors between approximately 1000 BCE and 700 CE—and argues that this millennium of Mediterranean integration did not result in civilisational replacement but in the first great instantiation of the Moroccan synthesis. The pattern of selective adoption, indigenisation, and institutional layering that would become systemic across subsequent centuries was already, in this early period, fully operative.

This argument challenges two entrenched historiographical tropes that have long distorted the study of North African antiquity. The first is the terra nullius model, inherited from colonial historiography, which imagined Phoenician navigators encountering empty coastlines and implanting civilisation upon a vacant periphery. The second is the romanisation-as-assimilation model, which assumed that indigenous society was passively absorbed into a superior imperial culture, losing its distinctive identity in the process. Recent archaeological and epigraphic research has rendered both positions empirically untenable. What emerges instead is a picture of managed contact, in which external commercial and political systems were selectively engaged by indigenous communities that retained substantial autonomy over their linguistic, legal, and territorial arrangements. This early synthesis, I argue, established the institutional template—modular resilience, legal pluralism, and strategic clientage—that would enable Moroccan civilisation to absorb far more disruptive shocks in later centuries.

