Chapter 4

Phoenician and Carthaginian Networks: The Atlantic Frontier of the Mediterranean World

The arrival of Levantine navigators on the Atlantic coast of North Africa in the early first millennium BCE has long been acknowledged in classical sources, but the nature and consequences of this contact have been fundamentally reinterpreted in light of modern archaeological research. The traditional narrative, drawn from Greek and Roman literary accounts, presented the Phoenician presence as a colonial implantation driven by the expansionist logic of Tyre and, later, Carthage. The material record now suggests a more complex and negotiated reality: a network of coastal installations that functioned not as settler colonies in the Greek or Roman sense, but as commercial interfaces between Mediterranean mercantile capital and indigenous African systems of production and exchange.

Classical textual tradition, from the periplographic literature of Pseudo-Scylax to the encyclopaedic compilations of Pliny the Elder, consistently emphasised the antiquity of Lixus on the Atlantic coast of present-day Morocco. Modern stratigraphic excavation has confirmed the depth of this occupation. The major early settlements associated with Phoenician and subsequent Carthaginian activity—Chellah, Lixus, and Mogador—were established along the Atlantic littoral by the eighth century BCE, with material evidence of sustained Phoenician presence at Lixus and Mogador from at least the seventh or sixth century BCE (Aranegui Gascó 2001; Ponsich 1982). Mogador, the island site in the bay of present-day Essaouira, represents the most distant Phoenician installation so far documented, marking the southwestern limit of the Mediterranean world-system's Atlantic extension (Desjacques and Koeberlé 1963; El Khayari 2014).

Lixus: The Sacred and Commercial Node

Situated on the Loukkos estuary near modern Larache, Lixus was never a simple trading factory or emporion of the kind established elsewhere on the North African coast. Its excavated stratigraphy reveals five major occupational phases spanning more than two millennia: Phoenician, Punic, Mauritanian, Roman, and Islamic (Aranegui Gascó 2001; Habibi and Aranegui 2019). The site's sacral landscape was structured around a temple complex dedicated to Melqart, the tutelary deity of Tyre, indicating that the Phoenician presence at Lixus was not merely a temporary commercial outpost but a fully articulated settlement with permanent religious and political institutions (López Pardo 2002). The cult of Melqart functioned simultaneously as a ritual anchor for the merchant community and as a diplomatic bridge to indigenous elites, who recognised in the deity a cognate of their own sacral kingship traditions. Ongoing Moroccan–Spanish archaeological campaigns continue to reveal the site's role as a major industrial and commercial centre, particularly for the production of garum, the fermented fish sauce that constituted one of the principal export commodities of the western Mediterranean economy (Aranegui Gascó and Vives-Ferrándiz 2014).

Mogador: The Purpurarian Frontier

The archaeological signature of Mogador Island, located in the bay of Essaouira, differs markedly from that of Lixus, reflecting its specialised function within the Phoenician commercial system. Excavations have yielded Phoenician red-slip pottery dated to the seventh century BCE, alongside extensive evidence of murex shell processing, confirming the island's role as a seasonal purpurarian installation—a facility dedicated to the extraction of the precious purple dye that constituted one of the most valuable commodities of the ancient Mediterranean economy (Jodin 1966; El Khayari 2014). The Mogador installation was almost certainly controlled by merchants based at the larger centres of Lixus and Gadir (modern Cádiz), and its operation was oriented toward the procurement of Atlantic resources: gold dust, ivory, ostrich eggshells, and, critically, access to the indigenous Amazigh exchange networks that channelled these goods from the African interior to the coast (Mederos Martín and Escribano Cobo 2011). The seasonal nature of the Mogador installation—its occupation likely limited to the summer sailing season when the Atlantic winds permitted safe navigation—underscores the logistical constraints under which Phoenician commerce operated and the degree to which it depended upon the cooperation of coastal and near-interior indigenous communities.

The Indigenous Matrix

A critical finding of recent archaeological and historical research is that the Carthaginian presence in the Maghreb was not colonial in the Roman sense of territorial annexation and settler implantation. Carthage established important settlements at Lixus, Volubilis, Chellah, and Mogador, but these were superimposed atop pre-existing Mauri polities whose material culture, settlement patterns, and social organisation long predated the arrival of Levantine merchants (Camps 1984; Modéran 2003). The Carthaginian presence was hegemonic and commercial in character, extracting commodities and channelling trade through alliances with indigenous chiefdoms that controlled the interior caravan routes linking the Atlantic coast to the Atlas highlands and the Saharan fringe. Punic became a lingua franca of the coastal zone, a language of commerce and diplomacy that facilitated exchange between Mediterranean and African systems, but the evidence from funerary and epigraphic contexts strongly suggests the persistence of proto-Amazigh vernaculars in domestic, ritual, and rural settings (Camps 1984; Modéran 2003). As Gabriel Camps and Yves Modéran have argued, the populations designated by classical authors as Mauri were, in significant part, speakers of what are today recognised as Amazigh or Berber languages. This linguistic continuity, extending from the first millennium BCE into the present, constitutes the earliest historical indicator of the institutional resilience that would define Moroccan civilisation across subsequent eras (Camps 1984; Modéran 2003).

The pattern that emerges from the Phoenician and Carthaginian period, therefore, is not one of replacement but of superposition. Commercial enclaves were established at strategic coastal nodes; indigenous political structures were not dismantled but incorporated into expanding exchange networks; and linguistic and cultural practices were stratified rather than homogenised. The synthesis that would characterise Moroccan civilisation for the next three millennia was already, in this inaugural period of external contact, taking its definitive shape.

