Chapter 5

Mauretania Tingitana Under Rome: Limited Empire, Intensified Economy

The Roman annexation of the Mauretanian kingdom in 40–44 CE, following the assassination of Ptolemy by the emperor Caligula, is conventionally narrated in the historiography of Roman North Africa as a moment of rupture—a violent transition from indigenous client monarchy to direct imperial administration. Within the complex adaptive systems framework that organises this dissertation, however, the Roman interlude is more productively understood as a phase of institutional recombination, in which pre-existing political structures, economic networks, and social hierarchies were reconfigured rather than obliterated.

From Client Kingdom to Province

The political sequence that culminated in annexation is well documented in the literary and epigraphic record. Following the Jugurthine War (112–105 BCE), the western Maghreb came under the influence of the Numidian kingdom, initially through Bocchus I, whose opportunistic betrayal of Jugurtha to Sulla secured Roman favour and territorial concessions (Sallust, Bellum Jugurthinum; Coltelloni-Trannoy 1997). The Mauretanian kingdom was subsequently reorganised as a client state under the direct rule of Juba II, a Numidian prince educated in Rome and a personal friend of the emperor Augustus, who placed him on the throne in 25 BCE as part of a broader strategy of indirect governance along the empire's southern periphery (Roller 2003). Juba II and his son Ptolemy ruled from the twin capitals of Iol-Caesarea (modern Cherchell in Algeria) and Volubilis (in present-day Morocco) until Ptolemy's assassination prompted Claudius to dissolve the client kingdom and divide it into two provinces: Mauretania Caesariensis in the east and Mauretania Tingitana in the west (Cassius Dio, Roman History; Fishwick 1971).

The transition from client monarchy to provincial administration was, however, far less abrupt on the ground than the political narrative might suggest. Recent scholarship has emphasised the degree of institutional continuity that characterised the early provincial period: the same Mauritanian aristocratic families who had served Juba II and Ptolemy reappear in the epigraphic record as priests of the imperial cult and flamines of the new provincial order, suggesting that Roman administration co-opted rather than eliminated the indigenous elite (Lefebvre 2016; Mugnai 2018).

Volubilis as Systemic Hub

The most important urban centre of Mauretania Tingitana was Volubilis, located in the foothills of the Middle Atlas near modern Meknès. This city was the administrative and economic nucleus of the province, and its archaeology provides the richest single body of evidence for the nature of Roman rule in the far west of the empire. Significantly, Volubilis did not begin as a Roman foundation: its origins are demonstrably indigenous, with occupation dating to the third century BCE as a substantial Mauri settlement before the monumentalisation that accompanied Roman provincial status (Akerraz and Lenoir 2002; Panetier and Limane 2002). The Roman city was, in a literal sense, built atop and around a pre-existing African urban tradition.

Recent economic archaeology has fundamentally transformed scholarly understanding of Volubilis's function within the imperial system. The fertile plains of the province produced a wide range of agricultural commodities—grain, olive oil, wine—that were exported to Rome and to the military garrisons of the Rhineland and Britain (Mattingly 1995). The work of Francesca Bigi has demonstrated the exceptional intensity of this production regime: Volubilis contains no fewer than 58 oil presses within a mere 40 hectares of urban space, a density of productive infrastructure that is unparalleled in the Roman West (Bigi 2017). These urban oileries, Bigi argues, created a distinctive model of wealth accumulation—urban, artisanal, and controlled by a mixed elite composed of Roman colonists, romanised Mauri, and a significant Jewish mercantile community whose presence is attested archaeologically from the Roman period onward (Bigi 2017; Akerraz et al. 2010). The olive oil economy of Volubilis was thus not a simple extractive operation imposed by external imperial capital; it was a complex, locally embedded system in which African, Roman, and Jewish actors collaborated in the production and circulation of value.

The presence of a Jewish community in Roman Volubilis is of particular significance for the synthesis thesis advanced in this dissertation. Epigraphic and archaeological evidence—including menorah-decorated oil lamps and Hebrew-inscribed funerary stelae—documents the existence of a Jewish population that was fully integrated into the commercial life of the city while maintaining its own distinct religious and cultural institutions (Stern 2008; Akerraz et al. 2010). This early Jewish presence established a pattern of structured pluralism—the coexistence of multiple communal identities within a single urban and political framework—that would become characteristic of Moroccan civilisation in subsequent centuries, finding its fullest expression in the medieval and early modern periods.

The Limes as Zone, Not Line

The southern perimeter of Mauretania Tingitana was not a fortified border in the manner of Hadrian's Wall in Britain or the German limes in Upper Germania. It was, rather, a porous zone of surveillance and managed contact, designed to monitor and regulate—but not to prevent—the movement of transhumant pastoralists and the circulation of goods between the Roman province and the independent tribal territories of the Atlas and the pre-Saharan zone. The Polish–Moroccan archaeological mission's discovery of a Roman watchtower on the southern perimeter of the province, together with the catalogue of military camps extending from Tamuda in the north to Sala (Chellah) in the south, reveals a system of limited territorial control that was calibrated to extract agricultural surplus and maintain surveillance without attempting the impossible task of governing the mountain and desert interiors (Rebuffat 1998; Akerraz and Camporeale 2021).

This limited-control model had profound consequences for the long-term trajectory of Moroccan civilisation. By concentrating its administrative and military resources on the fertile coastal plains and the urban nodes of Volubilis, Sala, and Tingis (Tangier), Rome effectively preserved the institutional autonomy of the Atlas and pre-Saharan zones. Indigenous tribal confederations, customary legal systems, and transhumant pastoral economies continued to operate largely outside the direct reach of imperial administration. When Roman authority in Tingitana contracted in the late third century CE—the province was formally abandoned south of the Loukkos in 285 CE—the institutional modules of the interior remained intact, available for recombination under the new political conditions that would emerge with the Arab conquest and the Idrisid state formation of the eighth century.

Roman rule in Mauretania Tingitana thus lasted formally from 44 to 285 CE in the south, but its institutional imprint—urban planning, latifundia agriculture, Latin epigraphy, and the Christian church—was geographically restricted and socially selective. Volubilis, significantly, persisted as a major urban centre into Late Antiquity and, in a striking demonstration of institutional continuity, briefly served as the capital of the Idrisid dynasty in the eighth century CE, providing a direct material and symbolic link between the Roman provincial past and the Islamic imperial future (Akerraz and Lenoir 2002).

