Chapter 6

Amazigh Kingdoms and the Management of External Contact

The political history of the Mauri peoples during the first millennium BCE and the early centuries CE—the sequence of kings, alliances, and conflicts recorded in Greek and Roman sources—has too often been treated as a marginal appendix to the grand narrative of Mediterranean imperial expansion. Within the theoretical framework of this dissertation, however, the Mauritanian kingdoms represent a laboratory of indigenous statecraft, in which the strategic imperatives of engaging with powerful external actors were systematically negotiated through a repertoire of institutional techniques that would prove durable across subsequent centuries.

Clientage as Agency

The career of Juba II, the scholar-king who ruled Mauretania from 25 BCE to 23 CE, is conventionally narrated as a story of cultural assimilation: the Numidian prince educated in Rome, author of learned works in Greek on history, geography, and natural history, the personal friend of Augustus who transformed his kingdom into a model of Hellenistic-Roman monarchy (Roller 2003). This narrative is not false, but it is incomplete. Juba II's adoption of Roman cultural forms, his monumental building programme at Volubilis and Iol-Caesarea, his patronage of Greek and Latin letters, and his marriage to Cleopatra Selene, the daughter of Mark Antony and Cleopatra VII, can equally be read as a sophisticated political technology—a strategy of clientage that preserved substantial territorial autonomy for half a century by translating indigenous concepts of sacral kingship into the idiom that Roman power could recognise and respect (Coltelloni-Trannoy 1997; Alexandropoulos 2007).

The court that Juba II assembled at Volubilis was, in all likelihood, a bilingual and bicultural space, in which Latin and Greek were the languages of diplomatic correspondence and monumental epigraphy while Amazigh vernaculars remained the languages of domestic governance, military command, and rural administration. The coinage issued by Juba and his son Ptolemy deploys a carefully calibrated iconographic programme: the obverse bears the portrait of the reigning monarch with Roman titulature, while the reverse frequently features indigenous symbols—the elephant, the palm tree, the Mauretanian horseman—that would have resonated with the kingdom's African subjects (Alexandropoulos 2007; Callegarin and Ripollès 2016). This is cultural synthesis operating at the highest level of political communication.

Insurgency as Negotiation

The limits of the synthesis achieved under Juba II and the early provincial administration are thrown into sharp relief by the revolt of Tacfarinas, the Numidian leader who waged a protracted guerrilla war against Roman forces in the Maghreb between 17 and 24 CE. Tacfarinas, a former auxiliary soldier in the Roman army who had learned Roman military techniques and then turned them against his former commanders, mobilised a coalition of tribal confederations whose grazing lands were threatened by the expansion of Roman latifundia agriculture (Tacitus, Annals; Raaflaub 2007). The revolt, as the Roman historian Tacitus described it in considerable detail, was not a simple act of barbarian resistance but a sustained insurgency that exploited the terrain of the Atlas and the pre-Saharan zone to wage a war of manoeuvre against a militarily superior opponent.

The Tacfarinas revolt is important to the argument of this dissertation not as an isolated episode of conflict but as a limit condition that reveals the structural logic of the system. When the imperial economic order proved incapable of accommodating customary land tenure—the collective grazing rights encoded in what later Amazigh legal tradition would formalise as izerf or azref—the tribal confederation activated its military module. The conflict was resolved, after seven years of intermittent campaigning, through a combination of military force and political negotiation that restored a modus vivendi between Roman agriculture and transhumant pastoralism. The institutional lesson is clear: the synthesis between imperial and indigenous systems was never a stable equilibrium but a dynamic negotiation, periodically destabilised by the expansionary pressures of the Mediterranean economy and periodically restored through the reassertion of indigenous territorial claims.

The Modular Resilience Thesis

The first millennium of intensive external contact on Moroccan soil, from the Phoenician landings of the eighth century BCE to the withdrawal of Roman authority in the late third century CE, thus established the fundamental institutional pattern that this dissertation identifies as modular resilience. Neither Phoenician commercial hegemony nor Roman imperial administration succeeded in replacing the Amazigh substratum—linguistic, legal, or political. Punic and Latin were superimposed as high-register languages of commerce, administration, and monumental display, while Amazigh vernaculars persisted as the low-register languages of household, pasture, and village. Roman civitas law, with its elaborate provisions for property, contract, and status, was introduced in the urban centres, but it coexisted with customary legal assemblies—the agraw that mediated disputes and allocated resources within tribal communities—rather than supplanting them (Camps 1984; Modéran 2003). Urbanism was intensified at Lixus, Volubilis, and Sala, but transhumant pastoralism and oasis agriculture continued under autonomous tribal governance across the vast majority of the territory.

When external pressure mounted—whether in the form of Carthaginian commercial demands, Roman fiscal exactions, or the violent dislocation of the Tacfarinas war—the civilisational system did not collapse. It deactivated the exposed module (the coastal client kingship, the Romanised urban elite, the latifundist economy) and fell back on the refugial modules that had been preserved in the mountain confederations and the oasis ksour. These refugial modules—linguistic, legal, and genealogical—conserved the institutional and cultural capital that would enable recombination when the external pressure subsided or when new political opportunities emerged. This is the mechanism that would enable the Idrisid state formation of the eighth century to draw upon indigenous Amazigh institutions even as it introduced Islam and Arabic as new high-register elements.

---

Conclusion of Part II

By 700 CE, on the eve of the Arab conquest of the Maghreb, the territory of present-day Morocco had experienced approximately one thousand years of intensive Mediterranean integration. Phoenician merchants had established commercial networks along the Atlantic coast. Carthage had exercised hegemonic influence over the coastal chiefdoms. Rome had incorporated the northern plains into its imperial system for two and a half centuries, extracting agricultural surplus and implanting urban institutions that would persist long after the legions withdrew. Yet none of these external engagements had succeeded in erasing the indigenous linguistic, legal, and political core of the region. The Amazigh substratum, far from being a passive recipient of Mediterranean civilisation, had actively managed the terms of contact, selecting, adapting, and indigenising external elements while preserving its own institutional autonomy across the vast interior spaces of the Atlas and the Sahara.

The mechanisms that enabled this resilience—corridor management that allowed indigenous communities to control the terms of exchange between coast and interior, legal pluralism that permitted Roman and customary systems to coexist rather than compete, and a strategic deployment of clientage and insurgency that maximised indigenous agency under conditions of unequal power—were, by the seventh century, already well established. The Arab conquest and the introduction of Islam, the subject of Part III, would constitute the most transformative external shock yet to reach the Maghreb. But the shock would not encounter a blank slate. It would encounter a complex adaptive system that had been practicing the art of managed synthesis for a thousand years and was structurally primed, in its institutional architecture and its cultural repertoire, for yet another cycle of incorporation, adaptation, and recombination.


PART III

IMPERIAL LABORATORIES (700–1554 CE)

Introduction: From Conquest to System

If Part II described the first synthesis under Mediterranean empires—the layering of Phoenician, Carthaginian, and Roman institutions atop an enduring Amazigh substratum—Part III analyses the second, more decisive synthesis: the Islamisation of the Maghreb al-Aqsa and the invention of its core political technologies. The period between the Arab arrival in the seventh century and the collapse of the Marinid sultanate in the fifteenth does not constitute a simple narrative of Arabisation or religious conversion. It is, rather, a series of institutional experiments—some failed, some remarkably durable—that generated the three modular institutions which have defined Moroccan statecraft into the twenty-first century: Sharifism, the Makhzen, and the Bayʿa.

We organise this analysis around Ibn Khaldūn’s theory of ʿaṣabiyya, the most powerful endogenous theory of civilisational cycles ever produced by the Maghrebi intellectual tradition. Ibn Khaldūn himself served as a court official and observer of several of the dynasties examined here, and his model of group solidarity, dynastic rise, and inevitable sedentary decay provides both a contemporary conceptual vocabulary and, when deployed critically, a framework for understanding the systemic dynamics of institutional experimentation, collapse, and recombination. The sequence of Idrisid, Almoravid, Almohad, and Marinid polities illustrates not a linear progression toward the modern state but a laboratory in which alternative configurations of authority, legitimacy, and administrative capacity were tested against the enduring constraints of Morocco’s four-corridor geography and its tribally organised human landscape.

By the end of this period, every major institutional module that would later be combined in the Saadian and Alaouite syntheses had been invented, stress-tested, and either discarded or retained. The Arab conquest had failed to produce direct caliphal rule but succeeded in generating a heterodox, Amazigh-led Islamic landscape. The Idrisids introduced Sharifism and the urban model of Fez. The Almoravids invented the Makhzen as a mobile, tax-collecting, tribal-integrative institution and founded Marrakesh as an imperial capital. The Almohads pushed ideological state-building to its limit, constructing a trans-Mediterranean caliphate grounded in a radical theology of tawḥīd. And the Marinids, in reaction, engineered the bureaucratic and educational infrastructure—the madrasa network, the dīwān, and the dedicated Makhzen city—that would enable subsequent dynasties to govern without perpetual recourse to revolutionary ʿaṣabiyya. The system, in short, had learned how to reproduce itself.

