Chapter 7

The Arab Conquest and the Islamisation of Amazigh Society: Rupture, Revolt, and Heterodoxy

The Umayyad incursions into the far Maghreb during the late seventh century did not produce stable administration west of Ifrīqiya any more than Roman occupation had produced territorial control beyond the Atlas. Geography and tribal organisation once again limited the reach of imperial power. The critical event of the early Islamic period was not the initial conquest but the Great Berber Revolt of 739–743 CE, a transformative insurrection that permanently altered the political and religious trajectory of the region.

The revolt was triggered by the fiscal and social discriminations imposed by Arab governors upon Muslim Berber converts, who were systematically treated as mawālī (clients) rather than full members of the umma, subjected to the jizya and other humiliating exactions despite their formal adherence to Islam. Khārijite Islam—with its insistence on the equality of all believers regardless of ethnicity and its rejection of hereditary caliphal authority—provided the ideological idiom of resistance. The outcome was decisive. Arab political control would endure in al-Andalus and Ifrīqiya, but the Berber hold on Morocco, as well as the western and central portions of the Maghrib al-Awsaṭ, would continue independently, leading rapidly to the establishment of autonomous Berber polities: the Barghawāṭa state in Tāmasnā by 744, the Emirate of Tlemcen by 742, and the Midrārid emirate of Sijilmāsa by 758.[^25fa]

The Barghawāṭa Paradigm

The Barghawāṭa confederacy, which ruled the Atlantic plain of Tāmasnā for more than three centuries (744–1058), is analytically central to any understanding of early Moroccan Islamisation. Far from being the heretical anomaly portrayed in later orthodox historiography, the Barghawāṭa represent an alternative model of indigenisation—an Amazigh Islam with its own prophet (Ṣāliḥ ibn Ṭarīf), its own scripture in a Berber language, and a deliberate synthesis of Islamic and pre-Islamic beliefs. Recent scholarship on "devout heretics" has demonstrated that the Barghawāṭa's image was progressively reduced to caricature in Mālikī and Sharīfian sources precisely because their successful, stable polity challenged the claim that legitimate Islamic governance required Arab lineage or caliphal sanction.[^46f0] Alongside the Midrārids of Sijilmāsa and the early Idrīsids of Walīlī, the Barghawāṭa demonstrate that the eighth and ninth centuries were characterised not merely by political fragmentation but by intense institutional innovation operating entirely outside the caliphal model. Islamisation did not mean Arabisation in any linear sense. It meant the adoption and creative adaptation of Islamic vocabularies of legitimacy to underwrite autonomous Amazigh polities.

The Geography of Resistance and Accommodation

The uneven geography of the conquest shaped the differential Islamisation of Moroccan territory in patterns that would persist for centuries. The coastal plains and major river valleys, where Arab garrisons were concentrated, experienced more rapid linguistic and religious transformation. The Roman towns of Walīlī (Volubilis), Chellah (Sala), and Lixus became nodes of Arab settlement and Islamic administration. Yet the mountainous interior presented a fundamentally different pattern. The Atlas ranges, with their difficult terrain and segmentary tribal organisation, functioned as zones of relative autonomy where Islamic influence penetrated slowly and selectively, carried by local murābiṭūn, Sufi lodges, and itinerant scholars rather than by state imposition. The result was a distinctive "Atlas Islam"—deeply devout but often operating outside the jurisdictional claims of urban-based Mālikī jurists, syncretic in its incorporation of pre-Islamic ritual elements, and organised around kin-based networks of religious authority that would endure into the modern period.

The pre-Saharan oases and trans-Saharan trading entrepôts, particularly Sijilmāsa, constituted a third zone of differential Islamisation. Here, Islamic identity was intimately bound up with commercial interests. The Midrārid emirate exemplifies how Berber elites could adopt Islam as a framework for state-building while maintaining extensive economic and cultural ties with sub-Saharan Africa. The Islam of Sijilmāsa was cosmopolitan, commercially oriented, and relatively independent of Maghrebi urban orthodoxies—a reminder that "Berber Islam" was never a uniform phenomenon.

This early period thus establishes a foundational principle for the remainder of this dissertation: against essentialist narratives of linear Arabisation or complete Islamisation, the evidence reveals a landscape of heterodox polities and regionally differentiated religious settlements. Islam provided a new high-register vocabulary of political legitimacy, but the institutional grammar into which it was inserted remained overwhelmingly Amazigh.

