Chapter 8

Idrisids to Almoravids: The Formation of the Makhzen

The Idrisid Moment (788–974 CE)

The Idrisid dynasty, an Arab Muslim dynasty ruling from 788 to 974, controlled much of present-day Morocco and represents the first successful attempt to construct a durable state by synthesising Arab-Islamic legitimacy with Berber political support. Its founder, Idrīs I ibn ʿAbd Allāh, was a sharīf—a descendant of the Prophet Muḥammad through his grandson al-Ḥasan—who fled to the Maghreb after the failed ʿAlid revolt at Fakhkh in 786. His arrival among the Awraba Berber confederation at Walīlī was not an act of conquest but of political refuge, and his authority depended entirely on the alliance he constructed with local tribal elites.[^cff6] The Awraba, who had previously supported Kusayla against the Umayyads, welcomed Idrīs not as an external conqueror but as a religious leader (imām) who could strengthen their political position against rival tribes and the residual claims of the Abbasid caliphate.

This alliance established a critical template for Moroccan statecraft: the ruler as a religiously legitimated figure whose authority was mediated through tribal consent, expressed through the Bayʿa, and continuously renegotiated through gift exchange, marriage politics, and military cooperation. Following the assassination of Idrīs I by an Abbasid agent in 791, the experiment was consolidated under his posthumously born son, Idrīs II (r. 803–828), who proved an astute political operator. He gradually transformed himself from a protégé of the Awraba into an independent sovereign by welcoming Arab immigrants, appointing Arab officials to key administrative and judicial posts, and relocating his capital from Awraba-dominated Walīlī to the newly founded city of Fes in 809.

The founding of Fes was a decisive act of state-building. The city was strategically positioned at the intersection of the Atlantic-Mediterranean corridor and the Atlas mountain zone, enabling control over transversal trade routes. Idrīs II welcomed two major waves of Arab immigration—in 818 from Córdoba and in 824 from Aghlabid Ifrīqiya—creating a cosmopolitan urban population that would make Fes the intellectual and commercial capital of the western Maghreb. The Idrisid period thus played a crucial role in the early Islamisation of Morocco and the Arabisation of its major urban centres, establishing a cultural duality between the Arabic-speaking, Mālikī-legal city and the Berber-speaking tribal hinterland that would structure Moroccan political life for over a millennium.[^cff6]

Yet the Idrisid state remained fragile. Upon Idrīs II’s death, the realm was divided among his sons, producing a constellation of competing emirates that progressively weakened the dynasty. The Idrisids were eventually crushed between the expanding Umayyad caliphate of Córdoba and the Fatimid caliphate of Cairo, losing Fes in 927 and final power in 974. The significance of the Idrisid experiment lies not in its longevity but in the institutional templates it bequeathed: the combination of Sharīfian descent as symbolic capital, urban patronage as a mechanism of state consolidation, and tribal alliance as the indispensable foundation of political authority. These elements would be recombined by every subsequent dynasty.

The Interregnum and the Rise of the Almoravids

The two centuries between the fall of the Idrisids and the Almoravid breakthrough (c. 974–1050s) were dominated by various Zenata Berber tribes in a loose network of principalities, a period of fragmentation that, from the perspective of Ibn Khaldūnian theory, represents the predictable decay of ʿaṣabiyya following the dissipation of Idrisid dynastic cohesion.[^f073] Yet this interregnum was also a period of institutional fermentation, in which local experiments—the Barghawāṭa confederacy, the Midrārid emirate, the Maghrāwa principalities—tested alternative configurations of authority and laid the groundwork for the next great synthesis.

The Almoravid movement emerged from this fragmented landscape as something genuinely novel: a trans-Saharan imperial project grounded in religious reform and the military capacity of the Sanhāja Berber confederation. The Almoravids originated among a coalition of the Lamtūna, Guddāla, and Massūfa, nomadic Berber tribes living in what is now Mauritania and the Western Sahara, traversing the territory between the Draa, the Niger, and the Senegal rivers.[^7b74] Under the spiritual leadership of the Mālikī scholar ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yāsīn and the military command of Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmar and Yūsuf ibn Tashfīn, this Sanhāja confederation transformed religious ʿaṣabiyya into imperial power.

The term "Almoravid"—al-Murābiṭūn, "those of the ribāṭ"—signals the movement's institutional innovation. The ribāṭ was not merely a frontier fortress but a total institution combining military training, religious education, and communal discipline under a rigorous Mālikī framework. It produced a new type of political actor: the militant scholar-soldier capable of both governing and fighting, loyal to a transcendent religious cause rather than to any particular tribe or territory. This institutional form enabled the Almoravids to project power across the vast distances of the Sahara and the Maghreb with a coherence that purely tribal confederations could not match.

Their most enduring act was urban and institutional: the founding of Marrakesh as a capital around 1070. More precisely, Marrakesh began in the 1060s as a Lamtūna tent city—a desert-style military encampment—and remained so until the first stone building, the Qaṣr al-Ḥajar ("Castle of Stone," the Almoravid treasury and armory fort), was erected in May 1070.[^7b74] This act embodies, in architectural form, the origin of the Makhzen.

Etymologically, the term makhzen originally denoted a warehouse or storehouse, and by extension the escort attached to the ruler or his deputy while they were outside the capital, particularly on maḥalla tours gathering taxes and provisioning the state. The Almoravid maḥalla—mobile, tax-collecting, grain-storing, treaty-making—became the institutional prototype for governing a tribally organised, ecologically fragmented territory. It created the classic duality that would define Moroccan political geography for a millennium: Bilād al-Makhzen, the "land of the Makhzen," designating areas under effective central authority where taxes were collected and justice administered, and Bilād al-Sība, the "land of dissidence," where autonomous tribal authority prevailed and state presence was episodic or symbolic.[^00b7]

The Almoravids thus solved the geographic problem identified in Part I of this dissertation: they linked the Saharan commercial corridor—seizing the trans-Saharan entrepôt of Awdaghost in 1054 and Sijilmāsa in 1056—to the Atlantic and Mediterranean corridors through a single mobile institution capable of aggregating resources from all four geographic systems. Under Yūsuf ibn Tashfīn (r. 1061–1106), the Almoravid empire stretched from the Senegal River to the Ebro, encompassing the western Maghreb and Muslim Iberia within a single political and legal framework.

The Almoravid synthesis, however, contained internal tensions. The movement’s original Saharan constituency remained committed to a rigorous, ascetic interpretation of Islam that sat uneasily with the urban luxury and cultural sophistication of Marrakesh and Córdoba. Their patronage of Mālikī scholarship created a class of jurists essential to imperial administration—al-Qāḍī ʿIyāḍ being the most celebrated exemplar—but their theological and legal orthodoxy left them vulnerable to accusations of anthropomorphism and spiritual laxity from more radical reformers. The very success of the Almoravid state generated the conditions for its ideological delegitimation.

