Chapter 9

Almohad and Marinid State-Building Experiments: Doctrine, Bureaucracy, and Khaldūnian Cycles

The Almohads (1121–1269): Doctrine as ʿAṣabiyya

If the Almoravids represented tribal ʿaṣabiyya institutionalised, the Almohads represented ideological ʿaṣabiyya institutionalised. The Almohad doctrine was the ideology underpinning the movement founded by Muḥammad ibn Tūmart, a Masmūda Berber from the Hargha tribe of the Anti-Atlas, whose theological studies in Córdoba, Baghdad, and perhaps the East had exposed him to a wide range of Islamic intellectual currents. Fundamental to Almohadism was Ibn Tūmart's radical interpretation of tawḥīd—"unity" or "oneness"—from which the Almohads derive their name: al-Muwaḥḥidūn.[^a540] He rejected Almoravid Mālikism as anthropomorphic and corrupt, and insisted on a strict, rationalist understanding of divine unity that required the rejection of all anthropomorphic conceptions of God.

In 1121, Ibn Tūmart proclaimed himself the Mahdī—the infallible, divinely guided leader who would restore justice and true religion to the world.[^2a31] This was not merely a religious claim but a revolutionary political act: a declaration of total war against the existing Almoravid order. The Almohad movement established its base at Tinmal in the High Atlas, creating a fortified commune that served as both spiritual centre and military headquarters—a deliberate emulation of the Prophet’s hijra to Medina. The organisational structure Ibn Tūmart constructed was unprecedented in Berber political history: at its apex the Ahl al-Dār (House of the Mahdī), surrounded by a Council of Ten (his closest disciples) and a Council of Fifty (representing the leading sheikhs of the Masmūda tribes). This structure transformed loose tribal solidarities into a centralised, ideologically coherent military-religious apparatus.

Ibn Khaldūn’s framework is essential for understanding the Almohad trajectory. He argued that their advent constitutes a break with the model of previous dynasties, particularly in the quality of their ʿaṣabiyya. For Ibn Khaldūn, ʿaṣabiyya is most potent when in constant motion—when it is directed toward a transcendent goal. When that motion towards power ends following the group’s political ascension, ʿaṣabiyya loses its telos, becomes sedentary, and slowly but steadily begins to decay.[^f35f] The Almohads illustrate this cycle with textbook clarity. The fierce mountain ʿaṣabiyya of Tinmal built an empire from Tunis to Seville under the caliph ʿAbd al-Muʾmin (r. 1130–1163) and his successors. The Almohad state was characterised by an unprecedented degree of centralisation: appointed governors, a standing army, an extensive network of fortresses, and an aggressive policy of religious standardisation that promoted the Ẓāhirī school of jurisprudence and persecuted Mālikī scholars who refused to conform. The architectural legacy of this imperial moment—the Koutoubia Mosque in Marrakesh, the Giralda in Seville—testifies to the scale of their ambition.

Yet, as the Khaldūnian model predicts, the Almohad ʿaṣabiyya institutionalised into a bureaucratic, Hispano-Maghrebi caliphate that burned heretical books and established imperial courts in Marrakesh and Seville as centres of instruction, and then began to fragment.[^f35f] The insistence on doctrinal uniformity alienated the Mālikī scholarly establishment. The persecution of non-Muslims disrupted commercial networks and drove valuable populations into exile—most famously the family of Maimonides. The Mahdist ideology that had fuelled the movement’s expansion proved impossible to sustain across generations. By the early thirteenth century, the caliphate was under pressure from Christian reconquest in Iberia, tribal rebellion in the Maghreb, and the rise of Sufi orders that appealed to popular religious sentiment. The Almohad experiment thus demonstrated both the extraordinary mobilising power of ideological state-building and its inherent fragility: a system too rigid to accommodate the pluralism of the societies it governed ultimately broke under its own weight.

The Marinids (1269–1465): The Bureaucratic Turn

The Marinid dynasty, a Zenata Berber group from the Figuig region, came to power by exploiting the exhaustion of Almohad radicalism. Their rise represents a classic instance of institutional consolidation following ideological overreach. In many respects, the Marinids reproduced or continued the social and political structures that existed under the Almohads, ruling a primarily tribal society through a council of tribal chiefs that maintained Berber traditions of consultative government.[^3dc8]

What the Marinids added, however, transformed the institutional landscape of Moroccan statecraft permanently. Their most significant innovation was the systematic deployment of the madrasa as an instrument of state-building. Between 1271 and 1355, they constructed dozens of residential colleges for the study of Mālikī law and related sciences, particularly in Fes, which they restored as their capital. The Saffārīn Madrasa (1271), the Ṣahrīj Madrasa (1321), the al-ʿAṭṭārīn Madrasa (1323), the Miṣbāḥiyya Madrasa (1346), and the magnificent Bū ʿInāniyya Madrasa (1350–1355) transformed Fes into one of the great intellectual capitals of the medieval Islamic world. The political function of these institutions was explicit: they trained the scholars and bureaucrats who staffed the expanding Marinid administration, creating a class of state-dependent elites whose loyalty was secured through salaries, housing, and the prestige of royal patronage. They counterbalanced the influence of rural murābiṭūn and Sufi shaykhs, whose popular authority could challenge the state, by creating an urban-based scholarly class committed to Mālikī orthodoxy and dynastic stability.

The Marinids also introduced a crucial spatial innovation: the construction of Fes al-Jadīd (New Fes) in 1276 as a dedicated administrative and military quarter adjacent to the old city. This created a dual urban structure that separated the ceremonial and bureaucratic functions of the state from the commercial and scholarly life of the existing metropolis—an architectural expression of the Makhzen’s increasing institutional differentiation from the society it governed. They formalised the dīwān bureaucracy, restored the ḥabūs (waqf) system as a mechanism of state patronage, and employed the great historian Ibn Khaldūn himself at their court, giving the theorist of dynastic cycles direct observational access to the system he would analyse.

The Marinid period therefore represents the formalisation of institutional modularity. By 1465, when the Marinids were overthrown, every module identified in this dissertation’s central thesis had been invented, tested, and refined: Sharifism to manage religious legitimacy, the Makhzen maḥalla to manage tribes and taxes, the Bayʿa to contractually bind elites, and the Mālikī madrasa to produce a literate administrative class. These modules could be detached from the specific dynasty that had created or refined them and recombined under new rulers—as the Saadians and Alaouites would subsequently demonstrate.

---

Conclusion to Part III: The Accumulation of Institutional Capital

The period from 700 to 1554 CE witnessed the progressive assembly of the institutional repertoire that would define Moroccan civilisation for the remainder of its history. The Arab conquest had failed to produce direct caliphal rule but succeeded in generating a heterodox, Amazigh-led Islamic landscape that demonstrated the plasticity of Islamic political vocabularies. The Idrisids invented Sharifism and the urban model of Fez. The Almoravids invented the Makhzen as a mobile, tax-collecting, tribal-integrative institution and founded Marrakesh as an imperial capital, linking the Saharan, Atlantic, and Mediterranean corridors. The Almohads invented ideological empire, pushing the limits of theological state-building and demonstrating both the power and the fragility of ʿaṣabiyya when it is made to carry a universalist doctrine. And the Marinids invented bureaucratic reproduction, constructing the madrasa network and the dīwān that would enable subsequent dynasties to govern without perpetual recourse to revolutionary group solidarity.

By 1465, the system was ready for the Atlantic-Saharan world-system shock that would define Part IV. The Saadian shurafāʾ would inherit not a blank slate but a richly articulated institutional ecology—a replicable system of statecraft that had been eight centuries in the making. The laboratory of imperial experimentation had produced its most durable result: not a particular state, but a modular repertoire of political technologies capable of being recombined under changing historical conditions. The resilience of Moroccan civilisation, in short, was no accident. It was the cumulative product of centuries of institutional learning.

