Part IV: The Atlantic-Saharan World-System (1554–1912)

Introduction: Morocco Between Two Oceans

The period from the mid-sixteenth century to the imposition of the French Protectorate in 1912 represents a distinctive phase in Moroccan civilizational history: the era in which the kingdom became fully integrated into emerging global networks of trade, diplomacy, and imperial competition while simultaneously consolidating its internal institutional architecture. This was not a passive encounter with modernity but an active repositioning. Moroccan elites—sultans, merchants, jurists, and tribal leaders—navigated the contradictory pressures of an expanding Atlantic economy and a venerable trans-Saharan system, extracting resources from both while preventing either from dissolving the autonomy of the Makhzen.

The concept of a "world-system," borrowed from Immanuel Wallerstein but applied here in a modified form, captures the structural reality of this period. Morocco did not merely trade with Europe and sub-Saharan Africa; it functioned as a critical articulation point between two distinct but increasingly interdependent economic zones. The Atlantic frontier, opened by Portuguese expansion and later exploited by Dutch, English, and French merchants, brought Moroccan sugar, leather, and ostrich feathers to European markets while importing firearms, textiles, and diplomatic recognition. The Saharan frontier, ancient in its origins but intensified by Saadian imperial ambition, channeled gold, slaves, and manuscripts northward while transmitting Islamic legal and scholarly traditions southward. The sultanate's prosperity—and ultimately its survival—depended on managing these twin flows.

This part examines four dimensions of this Atlantic-Saharan world-system. Chapter 10 analyzes the Saadian synthesis: the construction of a Sharifian imperial state that united the legacies of Marinid institutionalism with new military, economic, and diplomatic strategies, culminating in the decisive Battle of Ksar el-Kebir (1578) and the subsequent invasion of the Songhai Empire. Chapter 11 traces the Alaouite response to Saadian collapse, focusing on Mawlay Ismail's radical experiment in state centralization through the 'abid al-Bukhari army and the construction of Meknes as an imperial capital. Chapter 12 reconstructs the intellectual and commercial culture of Sijilmassa and the trans-Saharan book trade, arguing that manuscript circulation constituted a distinct form of civilizational connectivity that paralleled and sometimes exceeded the importance of gold and slaves. Chapter 13 examines the foundation of Essaouira (Mogador) as the pivot of Morocco's Atlantic reorientation, analyzing how Sultan Muhammad III engineered a new maritime frontier to capture European trade while preserving Makhzen control.

Throughout, the emphasis falls on institutional innovation under conditions of global economic transformation. The Moroccan state that entered the twentieth century was not a fossilized pre-modern formation but the product of successive adaptations to the gunpowder revolution, the Atlantic slave trade, the sugar boom and bust, and the diplomatic pluralism of the early modern world.

