Chapter 11: The Alaouite Dynasty and Institutional Consolidation

11.1 From Fragmentation to Reunification

The seventeenth century opened with Morocco in fragments. The Saadian civil war (1603–1627) devastated the central plains, encouraged European encroachment, and empowered regional strongmen—from the corsair republic of the Bou Regreg to the Sufi-led Dala'iyya confederation in the Middle Atlas. Into this vacuum emerged the Alaouites, a Sharifian family from the Tafilalt oasis claiming descent from the Prophet via his grandson Hasan. Their rise exemplifies the civilizational principle of institutional recombination: they adopted the Saadian idiom of Sharifian legitimacy while rejecting the Saadian dependence on tribal levies and mercenary forces.

The true architect of Alaouite power was Mawlay al-Rashid (r. 1664–1672), who reunified the country by capturing Fes in 1666 and Marrakesh in 1669. But it was his brother and successor, Mawlay Ismail (r. 1672–1727), who constructed the institutional framework that would define Moroccan statecraft for three centuries. Ismail inherited a country "weakened by internal tribal wars and royal succession struggles" and proceeded to impose a centralized authority unprecedented in Moroccan history. 

11.2 The 'Abid al-Bukhari: Slavery as Statecraft

Mawlay Ismail's most consequential innovation was the creation of the 'abid al-Bukhari, a professional army of sub-Saharan African slave-soldiers whose sole allegiance was to the sultan personally. The origins of this force lay in earlier Moroccan practice—the Almoravids, Almohads, Marinids, and Saadians had all employed black soldiers in various capacities—but Ismail transformed a auxiliary corps into the dominant pillar of the state. 

Ismail's method was radical and controversial. Beginning in 1679, he ordered the collection of all black Africans in Morocco—slaves, free Haratin, and even Muslims—into military service, a policy that violated Islamic legal norms against enslaving fellow Muslims.  By 1705, Moroccan registers recorded over 221,000 black Moroccans enlisted, though the actual standing army probably numbered between 50,000 and 150,000 at its peak.  The soldiers were called 'abid al-Bukhari because Ismail distributed copies of the Sahih al-Bukhari to their commanders, symbolizing both religious devotion and dynastic loyalty. 

The political logic was explicit. Tribal levies were unreliable because their primary loyalty lay with their kin groups; Sufi orders commanded spiritual allegiance that could challenge the state. Only a slave army, uprooted from their original communities and entirely dependent on the sultan for status and survival, could serve as a truly centralized instrument of power.  The 'abid collected taxes, suppressed rebellions, garrisoned fortresses, and even administered provinces. They became, in effect, the bureaucratic-military backbone of the Makhzen.

11.3 Meknes: The Imperial Capital as Total Institution

Mawlay Ismail's choice of Meknes as his capital was itself a political statement. Unlike Fes or Marrakesh, with their entrenched scholarly elites and tribal factions, Meknes was a relatively neutral space where the sultan could construct a royal city from scratch. Construction began immediately upon his accession in 1672 and continued for fifty-five years, employing paid workers, criminal prisoners, and Christian captives. 

The resulting kasbah complex—four times larger than the old city of Meknes, surrounded by 25 kilometers of walls, and containing multiple palaces, gardens, granaries, stables for 12,000 horses, and underground storage chambers—has been aptly described as the "Versailles of Morocco."  Its architecture blended Moroccan traditions with European influences, notably in the monumental Bab Mansur gate, designed by the renegade architect Mansur al-'Alj and adorned with Roman columns pilfered from Volubilis. 

Yet Meknes was more than an architectural vanity project. It functioned as a total institution—a self-contained administrative, military, and economic system that concentrated all threads of power in the sultan's person. The Jewish mellah was relocated adjacent to the kasbah to facilitate royal commerce; the 'abid were garrisoned in dedicated quarters; and the Christian prisoners who built its walls served as diplomatic bargaining chips for ransom and treaty negotiations.

11.4 The Alaouite State: Achievements and Contradictions

Ismail's reign achieved a degree of territorial consolidation and diplomatic recognition that no predecessor had matched. He expelled the English from Tangier in 1684, contained Spanish enclaves, and established regular diplomatic correspondence with Louis XIV of France.  He reasserted Moroccan suzerainty over the trans-Saharan routes and appointed governors deep into the Sahara. 

But the centralization was brittle. Ismail's heavy taxation and ruthless repression generated scholarly opposition; the Fes ulema, in particular, maintained tense relations with a sultan who violated Islamic norms regarding slavery and who ruled through force rather than consultation.  Upon his death in 1727, the 'abid became kingmakers, elevating and deposing his sons in a three-decade succession crisis that demonstrated how dependent the state had become on a single personality. 

The eventual restoration of order under Muhammad III (r. 1757–1790) required a fundamental reorientation. The new sultan disbanded much of the 'abid, curbed their power with tribal levies, and redirected state energy from military conquest toward commercial diplomacy.  This transition—from Ismail's coercive centralization to Muhammad III's mercantile pragmatism—established the template for Alaouite statecraft that would persist until the colonial period.

---

Chapter 12: Sijilmassa and the Trans-Saharan Book Trade

12.1 The Oasis as Civilizational Node

Sijilmassa, founded in the eighth century in the Tafilalt oasis of southeastern Morocco, was the most important commercial entrepôt of the medieval western Maghreb. At its height, it controlled the northern terminus of the trans-Saharan gold route, connecting the economies of West Africa—Ghana, Mali, and later the Songhai Empire—with the Mediterranean world. Archaeological and textual evidence suggests that the city minted gold and organized caravans that moved not only commodities but also ideas, technologies, and people across one of the world's most demanding geographical barriers. 

The city's significance for Moroccan civilizational history extends beyond economics. Sijilmassa was an early experiment in Islamic urbanism in the pre-Saharan zone, a place where Kharijite doctrine, Maliki jurisprudence, and indigenous Berber political traditions intersected. The eleventh-century geographer al-Bakri described its origins as simultaneously spiritual and commercial—a "spiritual refuge in the African desert" that became a hub of worldly wealth.  Its population of 40,000 at its peak was extraordinarily diverse, encompassing Berber merchants, Arab scholars, Jewish traders, and sub-Saharan African migrants. 

12.2 The Book Trade: Manuscripts as Trans-Saharan Currency

Recent scholarship has illuminated a dimension of trans-Saharan commerce that traditional historiography, fixated on gold and slaves, has largely overlooked: the trade in Arabic manuscripts. The edited volume The Trans-Saharan Book Trade: Manuscript Culture, Arabic Literacy and Intellectual History in Muslim Africa (2011) established that books constituted "the most profitable trade items in Timbuktu" and that their circulation created an intellectual commons spanning the Sahara. 

The mechanics of this trade reveal a sophisticated infrastructure of cultural transmission. Manuscripts moved northward from Timbuktu, Djenné, and Gao to Sijilmassa, Fes, and Marrakesh; southward from Fes and Cairo to Timbuktu and the Niger Bend; and laterally between Saharan scholarly centers such as Chinguetti and Walata. The carriers were not only merchants but also itinerant scholars, Sufi shaykhs, and pilgrims returning from the Hajj. Paper—manufactured in Egypt, Italy, and later locally—was itself a traded commodity, as were the inks, leather bindings, and wooden boards that constituted the material culture of the book.

The intellectual consequences were profound. The Maliki legal tradition that dominated Moroccan jurisprudence was continuously refreshed by contact with Sudanese scholarly centers. Timbuktu scholars such as Ahmad Baba, before his deportation to Marrakesh, maintained correspondence with Maghrebi counterparts on matters of law, theology, and grammar. Sufi orders, particularly the Qadiriyya and later the Tijaniyya, used manuscript networks to propagate litanies, hagiographies, and doctrinal treatises that bound together a trans-Saharan religious community.

12.3 Sijilmassa's Decline and the Persistence of Tafilalt Culture

Sijilmassa fell in 1393 after a prolonged period of political instability, and attempts to revive it in the seventeenth century proved unsuccessful.  Yet the civilizational system it had anchored did not disappear. The Tafilalt region continued to function as a corridor of connectivity, its settlement pattern shifting from a centralized walled city to a decentralized network of fortified villages (qṣūr) that maintained the trade relationships and kinship networks established during Sijilmassa's heyday. 

The Jewish communities of the Tafilalt, in particular, preserved a distinctive material culture that testified to this continuity. Marriage contracts (ketubah-s) from the region incorporated visual motifs—gates, quincunxes, Saharan landscape elements—that encoded the memory of trans-Saharan mobility and the cosmopolitan identity of an oasis society positioned between North and West Africa.  The Alaouite dynasty itself emerged from this Tafilalt milieu, and their eventual rise to power represented, in part, the reassertion of southeastern Moroccan commercial interests after centuries of northern dynastic dominance.

12.4 The Book Trade and Civilizational Resilience

From the perspective of complex adaptive systems, the trans-Saharan book trade exemplifies a critical mechanism of civilizational resilience: the maintenance of shared cognitive frameworks across political fragmentation. While dynasties rose and fell, while gold routes shifted and slave markets fluctuated, the manuscript networks preserved a coherent Islamic scholarly tradition that enabled the rapid reconstitution of state institutions after periods of collapse. The Makhzen's dependence on Maliki jurists for administrative personnel meant that the educational infrastructure of manuscript culture was simultaneously a political resource. When the Marinids built madrasas, when the Saadians patronized Timbuktu scholars, when the Alaouites restored Saharan trade routes, they were not merely engaging in cultural philanthropy but investing in the human capital that made statecraft possible.

