Part V: Modern Resilience (1912–2026)

Introduction: The Colonial Rupture and Its Aftermath

The imposition of the French and Spanish protectorates in 1912 constituted the most severe external shock to Moroccan civilization since the Arab conquest of the seventh century. Yet it was a shock of a fundamentally different character. Where previous encounters—Phoenician, Roman, Arab, Andalusian—had been assimilated through the mechanisms of synthesis and institutional recombination, colonial rule represented a direct challenge to the sovereignty of the Makhzen and the territorial integrity of the Moroccan state. France did not merely seek to extract resources or establish trading posts; it aimed to restructure Moroccan society according to European administrative, legal, and epistemological frameworks, while simultaneously preserving selected indigenous institutions as instruments of indirect control.

The paradox of the Protectorate—its simultaneous destruction and preservation of Moroccan institutions—has generated one of the most contested historiographical debates in modern Moroccan studies. Was the Protectorate a radical rupture that severed the continuity of civilizational development, or was it merely another layer in the long history of external influence being indigenized and recombined? This part argues for a more nuanced position: that the Protectorate was indeed a systemic shock Protectorate was indeed a systemic shock, but one that Moroccan civilization absorbed through mechanisms of selective adaptation, strategic resistance, and institutional subversion that had been refined across centuries of imperial encounters. The Makhzen survived not because the French wished to preserve it, but because Moroccan elites—sultans, tribal leaders, jurists, and eventually nationalist intellectuals—successfully navigated the contradictions of colonial rule to maintain the core modules of civilizational resilience.

The three chapters that follow trace this process through its major phases. Chapter 14 examines the Protectorate as a system of domination, analyzing how French indirect rule simultaneously dismantled and reinforced Moroccan state institutions, and how the nationalist movement gradually constructed an alternative vision of sovereignty that drew on both modern political ideologies and traditional idioms of legitimacy. Chapter 15 analyzes the post-independence period from 1956 to 1999, focusing on the tension between nation-building and Makhzen continuity under Mohammed V and Hassan II, and demonstrating how the Alaouite monarchy recombined colonial administrative structures with pre-colonial legitimacy mechanisms to construct a distinctive form of postcolonial authoritarian resilience. Chapter 16 examines the contemporary period from Mohammed VI's accession in 1999 to the present, analyzing the 2011 constitutional reforms, the institutionalization of Amazigh cultural recognition, and the Atlantic Initiative as the latest iteration of Morocco's millennial strategy of corridor-based civilizational connectivity.

---

Chapter 14: The Protectorate as Systemic Shock

14.1 The Architecture of Indirect Rule

The Treaty of Fes, signed on 30 March 1912, established a legal framework that was deliberately ambiguous. France assumed control of Morocco's foreign relations, military defense, and broad legislative authority, while the sultan retained sovereignty over "Moroccan or Islamic affairs" and continued to issue decrees in his own name and seal.  This fiction of protected sovereignty—what Resident-General Hubert Lyautey termed "the sharifian government, protected by the French"—was not merely diplomatic camouflage but a distinctive mode of colonial governance that would profoundly shape the post-independence Moroccan state. 

Lyautey's approach, often characterized as "indirect rule," was in fact more complex than this label suggests. He did not simply preserve indigenous institutions intact; he selectively reinforced, restructured, and instrumentalized them for colonial ends. The Makhzen elites—grand viziers, qāḍīs, provincial governors—were not displaced but incorporated into a dual administration in which French officials held effective power while Moroccan notables retained ceremonial authority.  The result was what Daniel Rivet has termed a "wary Malthusianism": a system that embraced selectivity as a matter of principle, carefully calibrating the degree of indigenous participation to maximize colonial control while minimizing the risk of organized resistance. 

This dual structure had profound consequences for Moroccan civilizational continuity. On the one hand, it preserved the institutional forms of the Makhzen—the sultanate, the Sharifian idiom, the Maliki judiciary, the tribal administration—through forty-four years of foreign domination. The same Grand Vizier, Muhammad al-Muqrī, who served under the sultan when the Protectorate was installed remained in office when Morocco recovered its independence in 1956, by which time he was reportedly more than one hundred years old.  This continuity of personnel, however symbolic, demonstrated that the Protectorate had not severed the thread of Moroccan state tradition but had rather suspended it within a colonial carapace.

On the other hand, the Protectorate introduced structural transformations that would permanently alter the Moroccan institutional landscape. The French created a modern bureaucracy staffed by European-trained Moroccan functionaries, imposed a new legal code (the dahir of 1913) that superseded customary law in many domains, constructed a transportation and communication infrastructure oriented toward European economic needs, and established an educational system designed to produce a class of indigenous intermediaries loyal to French interests.  Most consequentially, the French reconfigured the spatial organization of Moroccan cities, creating new European quarters (ville nouvelle) adjacent to indigenous medinas in a pattern of urban apartheid that would structure postcolonial social geography for generations. 

14.2 The Invention of Moroccan Islam

One of the most subtle yet consequential dimensions of the Protectorate was the French construction of a distinctive "Moroccan Islam" as an object of colonial knowledge and administration. Drawing on the ethnographic traditions of the nineteenth century, French scholars and administrators elaborated a vision of Moroccan religious life that emphasized its supposedly unique characteristics—Sufi mysticism, Sharifian veneration, tribal maraboutism—while marginalizing the universalist dimensions of Islamic doctrine that might sustain pan-Islamic or nationalist solidarity.

Edmund Burke III, in his landmark study The Ethnographic State, has demonstrated how this colonial invention of Moroccan Islam was not merely an academic exercise but a political technology that reinforced monarchical legitimacy and contained nationalist mobilization.  The French emphasis on the sultan as "Commander of the Faithful" (Amīr al-Muʾminīn), a title with medieval origins but renewed colonial significance, invested the monarchy with a religious aura that transcended its political subordination to the Residency. This construction would prove extraordinarily durable: the religious legitimacy that the French had cultivated as an instrument of colonial control became, in the post-independence period, the ideological foundation of Alaouite hegemony.

The Berber Dahir of 1930 exemplifies the contradictions of this colonial policy. Promulgated by the French administration with sultanic approval, the decree formally excluded Berber-speaking regions from the jurisdiction of Islamic law (sharīʿa), placing them instead under customary tribal courts and French colonial justice.  The official rationale was respect for indigenous custom; the practical effect was to divide Arab and Berber populations along legal lines, weakening the unified nationalist identity that was beginning to emerge in the cities. The dahir provoked a massive protest movement, including a fatwa from the scholars of Fes declaring the decree an attack on Islam, and became a catalyzing event for the Moroccan nationalist movement. Yet ironically, the very opposition that the dahir generated reinforced the centrality of the sultan as the symbolic defender of Moroccan Islamic unity—a role that would serve the monarchy well in the post-independence struggle for political dominance.

14.3 Nationalism and the Reconstruction of Sovereignty

The Moroccan nationalist movement emerged in the interwar period from a complex intersection of indigenous grievances, pan-Islamic solidarity, and modern political ideologies. The Istiqlal (Independence) Party, founded in 1944, became the organizational vehicle for a broad coalition of urban intellectuals, merchant elites, religious scholars, and tribal leaders who shared a commitment to ending colonial rule but diverged significantly in their visions of post-independence Morocco. 

The nationalist strategy centered on the sultan, Mohammed V, as the living symbol of Moroccan sovereignty. This was a calculated political choice that drew on deep historical resonances: the Bay'a as a renewable contract between ruler and community, the Sharifian idiom as a transcendent source of legitimacy, and the sultan's role as Commander of the Faithful as the defender of Islamic identity against colonial intrusion. When the French, in a catastrophic miscalculation, exiled Mohammed V to Madagascar in 1953 and installed the unpopular Muhammad Ben Aarafa in his place, they transformed a political dispute into a national-religious crisis.  The exile united urban nationalists with rural tribal populations in a common cause that transcended class and regional divisions—a pattern reminiscent of the anti-Almohad coalitions of the twelfth century.

The French decision to allow Mohammed V's return in 1955 and to negotiate independence in 1956 was driven not by military defeat but by the recognition that the colonial system had lost its ideological foundation. The sultan's return on 18 November 1955—commemorated as Moroccan Independence Day—represented not merely a political restoration but a civilizational reassertion: the reactivation of the Makhzen as a sovereign institution after four decades of suspended animation. 

Yet the independence achieved in 1956 was profoundly conditioned by the colonial legacy. France preserved significant influence, including rights to station troops and a say in foreign policy, while French settlers maintained their economic interests.  More importantly, the institutional architecture of the post-independence state—its administrative centralization, its legal pluralism, its educational system, its urban spatial organization—bore the indelible imprint of colonial design. The challenge for the new Moroccan leadership was not simply to expel the French but to indigenize these colonial structures, to transform them from instruments of foreign domination into modules of national resilience.

