Chapter 15: Independence, Nation-Building, and the Persistence of Imperial Institutions

15.1 Mohammed V and the Consolidation of Royal Power

The transition from Protectorate to independent kingdom between 1956 and 1961 was marked by a remarkable continuity of institutional forms masked by radical shifts in political substance. Sultan Mohammed V, who had become the symbolic embodiment of national resistance during his exile, assumed the title of King and set about consolidating a constitutional monarchy that would, in theory, balance royal authority with parliamentary representation and constitutional law. In practice, however, the dynamics of post-independence politics rapidly concentrated power in the royal institution.

The king's strategy drew directly on the civilizational repertoire refined over centuries: the Bay'a as a mass ritual of allegiance that periodically renewed the monarchy's popular mandate; the Sharifian idiom as a source of legitimacy that transcended party politics; and the Makhzen as a network of patronage, intelligence, and coercion that penetrated all levels of society. Mohammed V appointed himself prime minister, minister of defense, and minister of the interior, thereby subordinating the emerging party system to royal control.  The Istiqlal Party, which had expected to inherit the administrative apparatus of the colonial state, found itself progressively marginalized as the palace constructed parallel networks of authority.

This centralization was not merely the personal ambition of a monarch but a systemic response to the fragility of the post-colonial state. Morocco inherited a divided territory—the northern Rif zone under Spanish influence, the international city of Tangier, scattered French military bases—and a society deeply scarred by colonial economic exploitation and social disruption. The concentration of power in the monarchy was justified, both domestically and internationally, as a necessary measure to prevent the fragmentation that had befallen other post-colonial states. Yet it also established a pattern of political dualism—formal democratic institutions coexisting with substantive authoritarian practice—that would define Moroccan politics for decades.

15.2 Hassan II and the Years of Lead

The reign of Hassan II (1961–1999) represents the most intensive phase of post-independence state-building and, simultaneously, the most brutal period of political repression in modern Moroccan history. Hassan II inherited his father's centralizing impulse but pursued it with a ruthlessness born of existential insecurity. Two failed coup attempts in the early 1970s—one by military officers in 1971, another by air force cadets in 1972—convinced the king that no institution, including the army, could be trusted absolutely. The response was the systematic construction of a security state that penetrated every corner of Moroccan society. 

The political sociology of the Hassan II regime has been analyzed by scholars through various lenses. Guilain Denoeux and Driss Maghraoui have identified a "strategy of political dualism" in which the monarchy constructed a multiparty system designed not to facilitate democratic competition but to fragment the political landscape and prevent the emergence of any coherent opposition.  Parties were licensed, funded, and manipulated by the palace; electoral outcomes were carefully managed; and opposition leaders were alternately co-opted through ministerial appointments and suppressed through arrest, exile, or worse. The "Years of Lead"—les années de plomb—became the defining metaphor for a period in which thousands of political dissidents disappeared into secret detention centers, and the state's capacity for violence became the ultimate foundation of its authority. 

Yet even this regime of authoritarian terror operated within the civilizational framework established over centuries. Hassan II did not abolish the Bay'a; he theatricalized it, transforming the annual ceremony into a mass media spectacle that projected the image of national unity around the monarch. He did not suppress the ulema; he incorporated them, creating a state-controlled religious establishment that issued fatwas supportive of royal policy. He did not eliminate tribal structures; he manipulated them, using the system of rural caids and regional strongmen to maintain a decentralized network of surveillance and control. The Makhzen, in John Waterbury's classic formulation, remained the central institution of Moroccan politics—a "segmented" system of elite networks whose connections had been forged during the colonial era but whose logic derived from much older patterns of patronage and dependency. 

The 1990s witnessed a gradual liberalization driven by multiple pressures: economic crisis, the rise of political Islam, international human rights scrutiny, and the aging of the king himself. Constitutional reforms introduced a bicameral parliament, expanded press freedoms, and brought opposition parties into government.  Yet these reforms, however significant in comparative perspective, left the monarchy's dominance fundamentally intact. The king retained control of the military, the security services, and the religious establishment; the parliament remained subordinate; and the Makhzen continued to operate as the invisible infrastructure of power.

15.3 The Colonial Legacy and Institutional Bricolage

The post-independence Moroccan state thus presents a complex case of institutional bricolage in which colonial, pre-colonial, and modern elements were recombined into a distinctive configuration. The administrative apparatus inherited from France—ministries, provincial governorships, technical agencies—was staffed increasingly by Moroccan personnel but retained its hierarchical, centralized structure. The legal system maintained the dualism between French-inspired civil and commercial codes and the Maliki personal status law that the colonial administration had established. The educational system expanded dramatically but preserved the elitist selectivity that Lyautey had championed, producing a small class of highly trained technocrats alongside a mass of undereducated youth. 

The most consequential continuity, however, was the persistence of the Makhzen as the central organizing principle of the state. In Abdellah Hammoudi's influential analysis, the Makhzen is not merely a government but a "cultural foundation of Moroccan authoritarianism"—a master-disciple relationship that structures power at all levels of society, from the palace to the family.  This interpretation, while contested, captures an essential truth: the Moroccan state that emerged from colonial rule was not a European-style nation-state superimposed on traditional society, but rather a recombination of colonial administrative techniques with pre-colonial legitimacy mechanisms and post-colonial nationalist ideology. Its resilience derived precisely from this hybridity—from its capacity to speak multiple political languages (modernist, traditionalist, Islamist, developmentalist) and to deploy multiple technologies of power (bureaucratic, charismatic, coercive, patronage-based) in response to changing circumstances.

